1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,880
In a plane we located a point using distance
and angle.

2
00:00:02,979 --> 00:00:07,400
How can we describe positions and cameras when
height changes too?

3
00:00:07,760 --> 00:00:10,620
Part two introduces cylindrical coordinates
with

4
00:00:10,620 --> 00:00:13,420
height and spherical coordinates with two
angles.

5
00:00:13,720 --> 00:00:15,960
We will observe height and viewpoint in
gameplay,

6
00:00:15,960 --> 00:00:19,160
convert coordinates, and calculate a camera
position.

7
00:00:19,500 --> 00:00:22,160
Finally, we will examine axis conventions and

8
00:00:22,160 --> 00:00:25,180
boundaries where a direction is not uniquely
defined.

9
00:00:25,650 --> 00:00:28,790
Watch the bicycle riding and jumping in
Descenders.

10
00:00:28,990 --> 00:00:33,410
Separate its travel over the ground from its
height above the ground.

11
00:00:33,630 --> 00:00:37,470
Two coordinates on a plane cannot distinguish
jump height.

12
00:00:37,501 --> 00:00:40,270
In three dimensions we need another quantity
for up and down.

13
00:00:40,650 --> 00:00:44,770
The bicycle's position also differs from the
camera's position.

14
00:00:45,130 --> 00:00:48,110
The camera follows the moving subject from a
distance.

15
00:00:48,370 --> 00:00:51,810
We will describe these relationships with
cylindrical and spherical coordinates,

16
00:00:52,050 --> 00:00:54,730
without claiming to have inspected the game's
camera implementation.

17
00:00:54,730 --> 00:00:57,690
Before memorizing formulas, distinguish
horizontal distance,

18
00:00:57,690 --> 00:01:00,250
height, and viewing direction.

19
00:01:00,430 --> 00:01:05,370
Compare the moments before and after landing.
Ground travel continues while

20
00:01:05,610 --> 00:01:07,850
the bicycle is in the air. Read horizontal
movement and height separately.

21
00:01:08,150 --> 00:01:11,130
A subject remaining near the middle of the
frame is not necessarily stationary

22
00:01:11,390 --> 00:01:14,630
in the world. The subject and camera can move
together while retaining

23
00:01:15,050 --> 00:01:16,850
their relative relationship, so consider their
positions separately.

24
00:01:17,380 --> 00:01:23,020
Start with cylindrical coordinates.

25
00:01:23,020 --> 00:01:26,360
Here x and y form the ground plane, and z is
height. Check the axis labels.

26
00:01:26,560 --> 00:01:31,020
Locate the ground projection using r and
theta,

27
00:01:31,340 --> 00:01:34,820
then raise it by the chosen height. We have
radius, angle, and height.

28
00:01:35,100 --> 00:01:38,260
Radius r is horizontal distance, not total
distance from the origin to the 3D point.

29
00:01:38,720 --> 00:01:43,340
Height can change while r stays fixed.

30
00:01:43,480 --> 00:01:47,360
Keeping r fixed while varying angle and height

31
00:01:47,380 --> 00:01:49,760
traces a cylindrical surface, explaining the
name.

32
00:01:50,020 --> 00:01:54,800
Keeping the angle fixed with nonnegative
radius traces a vertical half-plane.

33
00:01:55,360 --> 00:01:59,040
Allowing negative radius includes the opposite
half as well.

34
00:01:59,750 --> 00:02:03,030
To convert cylindrical coordinates,

35
00:02:03,470 --> 00:02:08,250
reuse the cosine and sine formulas from part
one for the ground projection.

36
00:02:08,590 --> 00:02:12,115
Copy the height into the third coordinate.
Radius five, angle zero,

37
00:02:12,230 --> 00:02:16,950
and height three give Cartesian coordinates
five, zero, three.

38
00:02:17,550 --> 00:02:20,350
The full distance from the origin is not five.
Combining horizontal

39
00:02:20,650 --> 00:02:25,990
distance five with height three gives square
root 34, approximately 5.83.

40
00:02:25,990 --> 00:02:29,450
For the inverse, use hypot of x and y for
horizontal radius and

41
00:02:29,450 --> 00:02:33,790
atan2 for the ground direction. Keep the
height unchanged.

42
00:02:33,790 --> 00:02:37,150
At a zero horizontal projection, the angle is
not determined.

43
00:02:37,610 --> 00:02:40,950
A nonzero height does not define a horizontal
direction.

44
00:02:41,480 --> 00:02:43,160
Watch the ramps and jumps. The bicycle
advances

45
00:02:43,160 --> 00:02:47,240
along the track while also moving vertically.

46
00:02:47,620 --> 00:02:52,600
We can describe this by separating a
projection onto a chosen ground

47
00:02:53,060 --> 00:02:55,840
plane from height. That is the starting idea
of cylindrical coordinates.

48
00:02:55,840 --> 00:02:59,900
Changing height need not change horizontal
radius.

49
00:03:00,320 --> 00:03:03,100
Moving horizontally can also leave height
unchanged.

50
00:03:03,100 --> 00:03:09,460
The actual terrain is not flat. Our projection
uses a chosen coordinate plane;

51
00:03:09,460 --> 00:03:13,260
world height and clearance above the terrain
can differ.

52
00:03:13,520 --> 00:03:15,960
Identify the height reference as well as the
value to connect

53
00:03:15,960 --> 00:03:19,860
the mathematics accurately with the visible
action.

54
00:03:20,330 --> 00:03:24,570
Now use total distance from the origin and two
angles: spherical coordinates.

55
00:03:24,830 --> 00:03:29,410
First consider one common mathematical
convention.

56
00:03:29,690 --> 00:03:34,530
The z axis points upward. Theta is the
ground-plane angle from positive x;

57
00:03:34,670 --> 00:03:38,950
phi measures downward from positive z.

58
00:03:39,050 --> 00:03:44,930
Phi zero means the top, 90 degrees means the
horizon, and 180 degrees means the bottom.

59
00:03:45,270 --> 00:03:48,010
Do not mix this reference with other angle
definitions.

60
00:03:48,010 --> 00:03:54,750
Horizontal radius is r sine phi, and height is
r cosine phi.

61
00:03:55,070 --> 00:03:59,370
Multiply horizontal radius by cosine theta and
sine theta to get x and y.

62
00:03:59,930 --> 00:04:02,490
A fixed total radius places points on a
sphere.

63
00:04:02,910 --> 00:04:07,070
In this mathematical convention, theta is
atan2 of y and x. Phi can be obtained with

64
00:04:07,630 --> 00:04:13,950
atan2 of horizontal radius and z. Origin and
pole direction policies still apply.

65
00:04:14,050 --> 00:04:19,230
An elevation or latitude measured from the
horizon is 90 degrees minus phi.

66
00:04:19,690 --> 00:04:22,590
Total radius is not altitude above a surface;

67
00:04:22,830 --> 00:04:25,030
subtract the reference sphere radius to obtain
that altitude.

68
00:04:25,500 --> 00:04:29,720
Games often call the direction angles heading
and pitch,

69
00:04:30,100 --> 00:04:33,340
but engines do not all share the same axes or
signs.

70
00:04:33,580 --> 00:04:37,700
In this book's game convention, x points
right, y up, and z forward.

71
00:04:37,700 --> 00:04:42,900
Zero heading points forward; positive heading
turns right.

72
00:04:43,020 --> 00:04:46,880
Zero pitch is horizontal, and positive pitch
points downward.

73
00:04:47,160 --> 00:04:51,260
Looking upward gives negative pitch. We will
keep this sign convention visible.

74
00:04:51,480 --> 00:04:55,120
The mathematical convention measured from the
upward axis.

75
00:04:55,120 --> 00:04:59,740
Substituting heading and pitch directly into
that formula can locate a different point.

76
00:05:00,020 --> 00:05:03,040
Two angles describe direction, not a complete
orientation.

77
00:05:03,440 --> 00:05:08,880
Sideways roll needs additional information;
check the convention used by your engine.

78
00:05:09,470 --> 00:05:13,390
Watch the bicycle jump and rotate over the
ramps.

79
00:05:13,670 --> 00:05:18,210
Do not confuse the camera's viewing direction
with the bicycle's entire orientation.

80
00:05:18,450 --> 00:05:20,230
Where something points is a direction
question;

81
00:05:20,230 --> 00:05:24,230
how far it leans sideways is another part of
orientation.

82
00:05:24,370 --> 00:05:27,230
Heading and pitch can describe a direction,

83
00:05:27,470 --> 00:05:31,690
but not every rotation around that direction.

84
00:05:32,090 --> 00:05:34,830
A tilted horizon on screen also does not
establish

85
00:05:34,830 --> 00:05:37,030
that the world's upward axis has changed.

86
00:05:37,350 --> 00:05:41,970
For now, focus on position and direction. In
the 3D diagrams,

87
00:05:41,970 --> 00:05:45,210
check the axes first and follow what each
angle controls.

88
00:05:45,470 --> 00:05:48,350
Read the jump’s travel direction separately
from the bicycle’s tilt.

89
00:05:48,790 --> 00:05:51,930
It can continue traveling in one direction
while its body rotates.

90
00:05:52,270 --> 00:05:55,130
One direction vector and a complete
orientation are different things.

91
00:05:55,390 --> 00:05:59,190
Rising and falling on the screen is the result
of camera projection. Measure world

92
00:05:59,510 --> 00:06:05,630
height along the declared upward axis. Screen
y is not automatically world height.

93
00:06:06,400 --> 00:06:09,000
Convert using the book's convention by first
separating total radius

94
00:06:09,320 --> 00:06:13,680
into horizontal length and vertical
displacement using pitch.

95
00:06:14,040 --> 00:06:19,900
Horizontal length is r cosine pitch.

96
00:06:20,120 --> 00:06:23,260
The y value is minus r sine pitch because
positive pitch points downward.

97
00:06:23,400 --> 00:06:30,680
Multiply horizontal length by sine heading for
x, and by cosine heading for forward z.

98
00:06:30,860 --> 00:06:35,720
At zero heading and pitch, x and y should
vanish and only forward z should remain.

99
00:06:35,748 --> 00:06:38,160
At 90-degree heading, the point should be to
the right.

100
00:06:38,420 --> 00:06:45,140
Checking reference directions catches swapped
sine and cosine terms or incorrect signs.

101
00:06:45,380 --> 00:06:49,240
Radius uses world units; code angles use
radians.

102
00:06:49,890 --> 00:06:57,430
For the inverse, calculate total radius with
hypot of x, y, and z.

103
00:06:57,750 --> 00:07:02,650
Then find horizontal length from x and z.
Heading is atan2 of x first and z second;

104
00:07:02,650 --> 00:07:08,150
the axes differ from part one's 2D function.

105
00:07:08,370 --> 00:07:13,190
Pitch can be computed with atan2 of negative y
and horizontal length.

106
00:07:13,450 --> 00:07:16,690
This handles vertical sign and horizontal
distance together.

107
00:07:16,690 --> 00:07:20,490
At the origin, direction is undefined. At an
exactly vertical point,

108
00:07:20,970 --> 00:07:25,250
zero horizontal length leaves heading
undefined.

109
00:07:25,630 --> 00:07:28,830
For canonical storage, choose zero heading
there.

110
00:07:29,470 --> 00:07:34,170
Whether a live camera keeps its previous
heading is a separate behavior policy.

111
00:07:34,820 --> 00:07:40,000
In this dusk sequence, the camera follows the
moving bicycle.

112
00:07:40,240 --> 00:07:43,780
Camera elevation and separation affect the
composition.

113
00:07:44,040 --> 00:07:49,460
The image alone does not reveal the camera's
exact world coordinates or heading values.

114
00:07:49,720 --> 00:07:53,880
Distinguish the relationships: where the
subject is,

115
00:07:53,880 --> 00:07:58,840
where the camera is relative to it, and what
the camera looks toward.

116
00:07:58,907 --> 00:08:00,980
Subtract world positions and express the
resulting relative vector

117
00:08:00,980 --> 00:08:04,800
using radius and two angles to calculate those
relationships.

118
00:08:04,820 --> 00:08:07,740
Next, we will examine equivalent angle

119
00:08:07,740 --> 00:08:10,200
representations and how to choose a canonical
one.

120
00:08:10,340 --> 00:08:14,480
If the subject and camera travel forward
together, the subject can stay near the same

121
00:08:14,700 --> 00:08:17,900
screen position. This is why we distinguish
screen position from world position.

122
00:08:18,100 --> 00:08:21,040
Think of subtracting the subject’s position to
move the reference center.

123
00:08:21,280 --> 00:08:23,760
Split that difference into horizontal length
and vertical

124
00:08:23,760 --> 00:08:26,460
height to connect the observation with the
inverse formulas.

125
00:08:26,950 --> 00:08:30,470
Adding a full turn to heading or pitch returns
to the same direction.

126
00:08:30,770 --> 00:08:33,230
Spherical coordinates also have aliases.

127
00:08:33,470 --> 00:08:35,770
For negative radius, make it positive,

128
00:08:35,870 --> 00:08:39,770
add 180 degrees to heading, and negate pitch
to preserve the point.

129
00:08:40,150 --> 00:08:43,430
Another alias keeps radius unchanged, adds 180
to heading,

130
00:08:43,430 --> 00:08:46,970
and replaces pitch by 180 degrees minus pitch.

131
00:08:47,190 --> 00:08:51,470
Heading 90 and pitch 45 therefore describe the
same direction as heading minus

132
00:08:51,470 --> 00:08:57,170
90 and pitch 135. An unfamiliar representation
is not necessarily invalid.

133
00:08:57,390 --> 00:09:00,370
Convert both representations to Cartesian
coordinates

134
00:09:00,650 --> 00:09:04,770
to check that canonicalization has not moved
the point.

135
00:09:05,240 --> 00:09:09,460
A canonical form can use nonnegative radius, a
one-turn heading interval,

136
00:09:09,460 --> 00:09:13,160
and pitch between minus 90 and positive 90
degrees.

137
00:09:13,440 --> 00:09:18,500
Fold an out-of-range pitch using the alias
relationship while preserving the point.

138
00:09:18,800 --> 00:09:21,860
Reduce very large angles by full turns first.

139
00:09:22,220 --> 00:09:27,000
At the exact upward or downward pole, changing
heading does not change direction.

140
00:09:27,320 --> 00:09:30,220
Many headings describe that same vertical
direction.

141
00:09:30,460 --> 00:09:34,520
This is a singularity of direction
coordinates.

142
00:09:34,520 --> 00:09:38,720
Two direction angles do not fully explain
Euler-orientation gimbal lock.

143
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:41,580
Canonical storage can use zero heading;

144
00:09:41,580 --> 00:09:46,620
camera controls can retain previous heading or
avoid the pole.

145
00:09:46,900 --> 00:09:51,920
Snapping nearby points to a pole with a
tolerance changes their positions slightly.

146
00:09:52,570 --> 00:09:55,450
Watch the steep ramps. One position formula
does not

147
00:09:55,730 --> 00:09:59,570
determine the whole behavior of a following
camera.

148
00:09:59,830 --> 00:10:03,090
After calculating a target direction,

149
00:10:03,450 --> 00:10:06,710
choices remain: how quickly to follow it and
what vertical-angle limits to allow.

150
00:10:07,030 --> 00:10:11,470
Near a pole, a small position change can
produce a large change in the heading number.

151
00:10:11,970 --> 00:10:15,270
That does not mean the target moved along a
large arc.

152
00:10:15,590 --> 00:10:18,530
Canonicalizing stored numbers and producing

153
00:10:18,530 --> 00:10:21,010
smooth camera motion are therefore separate
tasks.

154
00:10:21,010 --> 00:10:24,810
This recording does not establish which limits
the game uses.

155
00:10:25,310 --> 00:10:28,850
We are identifying decisions to examine when
building our own camera.

156
00:10:29,660 --> 00:10:34,700
Calculate an orbit camera around center zero,
one, zero,

157
00:10:34,700 --> 00:10:39,460
with radius five, heading 60 degrees, and
pitch minus 30 degrees.

158
00:10:39,504 --> 00:10:46,120
The book's formula gives offset x 3.750, y
2.500, and z approximately 2.165.

159
00:10:46,460 --> 00:10:50,980
Add the center to obtain camera position
approximately 3.750,

160
00:10:51,140 --> 00:10:55,960
3.500, 2.165. This offset points from the
center to the camera.

161
00:10:55,960 --> 00:11:00,500
To look at the center, subtract the camera
position from the center instead.

162
00:11:01,040 --> 00:11:03,640
Confusing these opposite directions can make
the camera face away.

163
00:11:04,220 --> 00:11:08,300
Use radius and two angles to place the camera,
then calculate its viewing direction

164
00:11:08,660 --> 00:11:13,020
separately. Pitch limits and smooth following
are additional control rules.

165
00:11:13,950 --> 00:11:19,130
Recall the camera's relation to the bicycle:
behind and above.

166
00:11:20,130 --> 00:11:22,730
We are not measuring an exact distance from
this recording.

167
00:11:23,050 --> 00:11:27,630
The camera-placement offset points from the
subject to the camera.

168
00:11:27,990 --> 00:11:30,470
The viewing direction points the opposite way.

169
00:11:30,770 --> 00:11:34,230
Subtraction order reverses the vector between
the same two points. Place the camera

170
00:11:34,630 --> 00:11:39,890
using center plus offset, then calculate the
view direction using center minus camera.

171
00:11:39,890 --> 00:11:42,370
When the subject moves, update the center and
optionally

172
00:11:42,370 --> 00:11:47,890
smooth the camera's movement toward its
desired placement.

173
00:11:48,290 --> 00:11:51,850
Spherical coordinates do not complete the
entire camera system automatically.

174
00:11:52,090 --> 00:11:54,710
They make its placement easier to express.

175
00:11:55,070 --> 00:11:58,150
As the ride continues, think about the center
moving through the world.

176
00:11:58,550 --> 00:12:03,810
The subject and camera can travel together
while keeping a similar relative offset.

177
00:12:04,150 --> 00:12:07,090
During a jump, also consider whether the
subject becomes obscured. Calculate the

178
00:12:07,090 --> 00:12:12,010
desired arrangement mathematically, then add
policies for obstacles and tracking speed.

179
00:12:12,653 --> 00:12:16,180
A 3D vector can use length and two direction
angles. For addition,

180
00:12:16,460 --> 00:12:22,640
convert to Cartesian components rather than
adding radii and angles separately.

181
00:12:22,920 --> 00:12:25,940
Handle the origin and exact poles explicitly.

182
00:12:26,220 --> 00:12:31,920
Decide how to treat tiny remaining horizontal
components according to the acceptable error.

183
00:12:32,260 --> 00:12:38,440
Another pitch formula uses arcsine. Rounding
can push a ratio slightly beyond one,

184
00:12:38,740 --> 00:12:41,660
so clamp its input to the interval from minus
one to one.

185
00:12:41,660 --> 00:12:46,160
The atan2 and hypot approach reduces this
division issue.

186
00:12:46,580 --> 00:12:52,040
But no formula restores precision already lost
in an enormous floating-point angle.

187
00:12:52,480 --> 00:12:57,340
Record axes and units, then test values near
zero and angle boundaries.

188
00:12:57,740 --> 00:13:01,500
Distinguish exact mathematical relationships
from computer approximations.

189
00:13:02,290 --> 00:13:07,090
Separate the bicycle's position, its leaning
orientation, and the camera viewpoint.

190
00:13:07,410 --> 00:13:11,490
One coordinate representation does not
determine all of them.

191
00:13:11,730 --> 00:13:16,870
Polar and spherical forms help calculate
distance and direction.

192
00:13:16,870 --> 00:13:21,190
Collision, orientation, and tracking speed
need additional rules.

193
00:13:21,390 --> 00:13:22,970
Read the visible actions separately and apply
these coordinates

194
00:13:22,970 --> 00:13:26,510
to the relationships involving distance and
direction.

195
00:13:26,730 --> 00:13:31,150
That makes both the usefulness and the scope
of each formula clear.

196
00:13:31,620 --> 00:13:35,910
Check three reference directions in the book's
convention.

197
00:13:36,120 --> 00:13:40,620
Radius two with zero heading and pitch gives
forward point zero, zero, two.

198
00:13:40,960 --> 00:13:44,303
Changing heading to 90 degrees gives rightward
point two,

199
00:13:44,520 --> 00:13:48,160
zero, zero. This checks the sine and cosine
order.

200
00:13:48,440 --> 00:13:52,680
Pitch minus 90 degrees gives upward point
zero, two, zero.

201
00:13:52,920 --> 00:13:55,900
A floating-point calculation may leave tiny
horizontal components.

202
00:13:56,080 --> 00:13:59,720
At that exact vertical direction, changing
heading reaches the same point.

203
00:13:59,720 --> 00:14:04,160
Recall why origin and pole storage and control
policies must be explicit.

204
00:14:04,600 --> 00:14:10,160
Cylindrical form adds height to ground radius
and angle;

205
00:14:10,460 --> 00:14:13,340
spherical form uses total radius and two
angles. Declare axes and units,

206
00:14:13,640 --> 00:14:15,980
then convert and verify before applying them
to positions or cameras.
